course: french
lesson: 32
title: Christmas, Epiphany and pancake day French-style
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Winter in France is full of feasts, and many of them look a little different from Christmas and pancake day in Britain, Ireland or America.
explain: In many French families the big Christmas meal is on the evening of 24 December, le réveillon, and children traditionally leave their shoes by the tree or the fireplace for le Père Noël, not stockings.
explain: Picture early January in a French classroom: a round, flat, golden cake is cut, the youngest child hides under the table and calls out who gets each slice, and somebody bites into a tiny hidden figure - la fève - and is crowned king for the day.
explain: On 2 February, la Chandeleur, French kitchens fill with crêpes, and some people flip them holding a coin in the other hand for luck all year.
explain: Then comes Mardi gras, "fat Tuesday", the last day of rich food before Lent, when French school children often dress up for carnival.
fact: Le réveillon de Noël is the big Christmas Eve dinner on 24 December; some families open presents that night and others on the morning of the 25th.
fact: Traditionally French children leave their shoes by the tree (le sapin) or the fireplace (la cheminée) for le Père Noël to fill with presents (les cadeaux).
fact: La bûche de Noël is a rolled chocolate cake shaped like a log; une bûche is a log for the fire, and the word is feminine.
fact: Strasbourg, in Alsace in the east of France, has one of the oldest Christmas markets in Europe, le marché de Noël, with gingerbread called le pain d'épices.
fact: For Epiphany, around 6 January, French families share la galette des rois, a puff-pastry cake with almond cream and a little charm called la fève hidden inside.
fact: Whoever finds la fève wears a paper crown, la couronne, and is king or queen for the day; you must eat slowly so you don't swallow it.
fact: The youngest child sits under the table and says who gets each slice, so nobody can cheat by choosing the slice with the fève.
fact: In the south of France people often eat le gâteau des rois instead, a brioche ring with candied fruit, which also hides a fève.
fact: La Chandeleur, 2 February, is the day for crêpes in France.
fact: Mardi gras means "fat Tuesday" (gras means fat); it was the last day for rich food before Lent (the weeks before Easter when many Christians ate plainer food), and children often wear costumes for carnival around then.
fact: Il est né, le divin enfant is a traditional French Christmas carol, hundreds of years old: "He is born, the divine child".
fact: At New Year people say "Bonne année, bonne santé !" - happy New Year and good health - and many send cards in January.
exercise: When is le réveillon de Noël? | On the evening of 24 December | On the morning of 25 December | On the evening of 31 December | On the morning of 6 January
exercise: What do French children traditionally leave out for le Père Noël? | Their shoes | Their stockings | A pillowcase | A plate of mince pies
exercise: What is le sapin? | The Christmas tree | The fireplace by the tree | The present | The paper crown
exercise: Jack eats la bûche de Noël. What is it? | A chocolate cake shaped like a log | A round almond cake with a hidden toy | A brioche ring with sugary fruit | A pile of thin pancakes with jam
exercise: Which is right? | la bûche | le bûche | les bûche | l'bûche
exercise: Which French city has one of the oldest Christmas markets in Europe? | Strasbourg | Marseille | Montpellier | Bordeaux
exercise: What is le pain d'épices? | Gingerbread | French bread | A hot drink | A Christmas log
exercise: When do French families eat la galette des rois? | Around 6 January, for Epiphany | On 24 December | On 2 February, for la Chandeleur | On 14 July
exercise: What is la fève? | A small charm hidden in the cake | The paper crown worn by the king | The almond cream inside the cake | The slice given to the youngest
exercise: What happens to the person who finds la fève? | They are king or queen for the day | They must hide under the table until the end | They have to bake next year's cake for everyone | They get the last slice and wash up the plates
exercise: Why does the youngest child sit under the table? | So nobody can cheat | They were naughty | To look for the fève if it falls on the floor | Because there is no chair left for them
exercise: Ollie in Marseille finds a brioche ring, not a flat cake. What is it? | le gâteau des rois, eaten in the south | la bûche de Noël, eaten at Christmas | la galette des rois, eaten in Paris | une crêpe, eaten at la Chandeleur
exercise: What do French people eat at la Chandeleur? | Crêpes | Galettes des rois | Snails | Gingerbread
exercise: What does Mardi gras mean? | Fat Tuesday | Mad Tuesday | Grand Tuesday | Grass Tuesday
exercise: What is "Il est né, le divin enfant"? | A traditional Christmas carol | A greeting for the New Year | A song for pancake day | A children's counting rhyme
exercise: What do people add to "Bonne année" in January? | bonne santé, good health | bon appétit, enjoy your meal | bonne nuit, good night | bon voyage, have a good trip
test: Charlotte from Kent goes, "They have the big dinner the night before? Bit early, innit?" What's true? | Yes, in many families it's on the 24th | No, French Christmas dinner is always on the 26th | No, the French don't eat anything special | Yes, but only in Quebec, never in France
test: Mia from Ohio says, "Shoes by the tree? That's lowkey sus." (sus = suspicious) What's the real story? | It's a French tradition: le Père Noël fills them | French kids hide their shoes from Santa | Shoes are a present for the reindeer | It's to keep the floor clean for Santa
test: Jack from Glasgow says, "Le bûche is brilliant, ken." Fix the little word. | la bûche, because bûche is feminine | le bûche is right, cakes are masculine | les bûche, because there are many slices | l'bûche, because it starts with b
test: Ethan from Boston drinks hot apple juice in Strasbourg and says, "Wicked festive, but Christmas markets are only German, right?" What do you tell him? | Strasbourg's market is one of the oldest in Europe | He's right, France has no Christmas markets | Strasbourg is in Germany, so he's right | The French copied markets from America
test: Lucas from Leeds asks, "Is the fève a choking hazard, though?" What is the sensible answer? | It's small, so you eat slowly and carefully | It's made of chocolate, so you can swallow it | It's never inside the cake, it's on top | It's a real bean, so it's healthy
test: Ava from Cork goes, "Sure why's the wee one under the table? Did they get told off?" Explain. | No, they say who gets each slice | Yes, the youngest is always punished at Epiphany | No, they are looking for a lost shoe | Yes, they ate the fève too early
test: What would happen if you flipped a crêpe at la Chandeleur with a coin in your other hand, according to the tradition? | You'd have luck all year | It'd turn to gold | You'd have to pay for the crêpes | You'd become king for the day
test: Grace from Toronto says, "Pancake day on 2 February? Eh, it's not even Lent!" What's true? | La Chandeleur on 2 February is France's big crêpe day | The French have no pancake day at all | The French eat pancakes only at Christmas | 2 February is Mardi gras in France
test: Harry from Swansea asks, "Fat Tuesday? Why's Tuesday fat, like?" Which is the best answer? | It was the last day for rich food before Lent | People eat only fat on that Tuesday | It's the longest Tuesday of the whole year | It's when the shops have a big sale
test: ★★★ A French family eats la galette with five people. The youngest is under the table. How many slices does the youngest call out names for? | Five, one for each person including the youngest | Four, because the youngest gets none | Six, one extra for the fève | One, only their own slice
test: Ruby from Brighton hears "Il est né, le divin enfant" and goes, "Bare old song, innit?" (bare = very) Is she right? | Yes, it's hundreds of years old | No, it was written last year for a film | No, it's a pop song from the 1990s | Yes, but it's sung only at Easter
test: ★★★ A galette is cut into 6 slices with one fève inside. Mum gets the first slice and there is no fève in it. How many slices could still hide it? | 5 | 6 | 4 | 1
exam: Identify the occasion on which, in many French households, the principal Christmas meal is consumed. | The evening of 24 December | The evening of 25 December | The morning of 26 December | The evening of 6 January
exam: State the function of the fève concealed within the galette des rois. | It designates the king or queen for the day | It designates the person who shall serve the cake | It designates the person who shall pay for the cake | It serves merely to decorate the upper surface
exam: Determine the date upon which la Chandeleur is observed. | 2 February | 6 January | 24 December | 1 April
exam: Ascertain the literal meaning of the expression "Mardi gras". | Fat Tuesday | Mad Tuesday | Last Tuesday | Great Tuesday
